[medium] internal/service/llm/anthropic.go:150 (correctness)
CompleteStructured creates its own request timeout, and the Anthropic SDK returns context.DeadlineExceeded when that timeout fires. mapAnthropicError returns context.DeadlineExceeded unchanged, so a provider/client timeout bypasses the exported sentinel retry contract even though SDK retries are disabled and the later River worker depends on these sentinels for retry/discard decisions.
Suggested fix: Distinguish caller cancellation from the wrapper-owned timeout. For example, when the parent ctx is not canceled but the call/request timeout expires, wrap the error with ErrOverloaded; continue returning the caller's context cancellation/deadline unchanged when ctx.Err() is non-nil. Add a test with a slow httptest server and a short RequestTimeout asserting errors.Is(err, ErrOverloaded).
Guidance: In internal/service/llm/anthropic.go, fix CompleteStructured/mapAnthropicError so the timeout created by `context.WithTimeout(ctx, c.requestTimeout)` is classified as retryable instead of returning `context.DeadlineExceeded` unchanged. The low-risk approach is to change the call to `mapAnthropicError(ctx, callCtx, err)` and change the helper signature to accept both the caller context and wrapper call context. In the context error branch, first check `if parentErr := ctx.Err(); parentErr != nil { return parentErr }` so caller cancellation or caller-owned deadline still passes through unchanged. Then check `if callCtx.Err() != nil { return fmt.Errorf("%w: %v", ErrOverloaded, err) }` so the wrapper-owned request timeout participates in the exported sentinel retry contract. Leave all existing Anthropic HTTP mappings intact: 400 stays ErrInvalidOutput, 429 stays ErrRateLimited, retryable HTTP statuses stay ErrOverloaded, and SDK retries must remain disabled. Do not solve this by removing `context.WithTimeout` or by mapping every context.Canceled/DeadlineExceeded to ErrOverloaded; caller cancellation must not be retried. Add a test in internal/service/llm/anthropic_test.go, e.g. `TestAnthropicClientRequestTimeoutIsRetryable`, using an httptest server that sleeps longer than a very short `RequestTimeout` (for example 10ms sleep vs 1ms or 5ms timeout), then assert `require.ErrorIs(t, err, ErrOverloaded)`. Also add or preserve a separate caller-canceled context test if needed to prove a pre-canceled parent context still returns `context.Canceled` and not ErrOverloaded.
